#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176107 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a52e1346-75c4-537c-add9-777911dbe5ff" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Spherical Representation of a Surface From an Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e0ce857-c6e5-508c-9b5f-a693b544a09f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A sphere" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173305} {:answer/id #uuid "8f30e618-a78c-5dd8-b83c-e37ab48a61a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A half-cone" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173306} {:answer/id #uuid "9c45273c-3962-5426-a077-000e741eeb95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173307} {:answer/id #uuid "c4f2dc5e-1674-527c-9261-c1dcdb1441a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A circular cylinder" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173308} {:answer/id #uuid "ce6c1174-e46d-5f48-91df-6d2f81c76e16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A plane that is parallel to the $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173309}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f30e618-a78c-5dd8-b83c-e37ab48a61a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A half-cone" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173306} :answer-field/id #uuid "1aca4911-d4fb-5ce9-a19e-4042412d8647" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173304}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a074f62e-9d2b-56d1-8816-21f9c254bcdf" :question/math-academy-id "q-102945" :question/problem "What surface is defined by the equation $ϕ=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ in spherical polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\nϕ=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nThis equation specifies that for any point on the surface, the angle that the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis must equal $\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ On the other hand, $\\rho $ and $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, since $\\rho \\ge 0,$ our surface is a half-cone.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069421/assets/27f9b580436d8e1aebeb4f150643a22266e9ad7f488b98e63f8a49dd4f1aa97f.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361620 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a52e1346-75c4-537c-add9-777911dbe5ff" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Spherical Representation of a Surface From an Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bfd184af-c443-5ce5-a494-704438845a83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A paraboloid" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361622} {:answer/id #uuid "eca197b7-b7b0-58b5-bd1e-a520132f4d42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A half-line" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361623} {:answer/id #uuid "5d2485ae-0fe9-5720-8d7d-f80a1a44c22c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A half-plane that passes through the $x$-axis" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361624} {:answer/id #uuid "9554a28e-acbf-52a5-8994-f2259ae5c2f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361625} {:answer/id #uuid "21ee4685-5180-5684-b220-d02334d56980" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A circular cylinder" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361626}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9554a28e-acbf-52a5-8994-f2259ae5c2f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361625} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee3233f5-30ea-5c0d-9fc0-bf2017770155" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361621}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "91c21edd-cba7-59db-be76-718b72384d4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-135302" :question/problem "What surface is defined by the equation $\\theta =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ in spherical polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nThis equation specifies that for any point on the surface, the angle that the corresponding vector makes with the positive $x$-axis must equal $\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.$ On the other hand, $\\rho $ and $ϕ$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, since $\\rho \\ge 0,$ our surface is a half-plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069421/assets/0043ffdc885e7edeebb7c4df571466fe4f13dfb08728290cd6d54f47777a7179.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361627 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fde60ded-ec05-5a2c-8844-26e8e02c9afb" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Part of a Sphere in Spherical Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53dab1c5-4f5e-5f48-a010-deadb89f38f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi /2,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361629} {:answer/id #uuid "9841a52b-29ab-50fd-82c1-42d60649c513" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi /2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361630} {:answer/id #uuid "a5864725-e578-53a6-9cc9-b1442aee87d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361631} {:answer/id #uuid "8cc51784-95a4-5419-9cc3-e21f84e37fc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361632} {:answer/id #uuid "bc18e2b0-42b8-594e-9c3d-2be82a719342" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le 2\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361633}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9841a52b-29ab-50fd-82c1-42d60649c513" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi /2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361630} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a4917d1-b91b-5846-81aa-8c90d0c79594" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361628}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9eae7513-d500-5ef8-82e2-f071bccc92fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-161803" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069421/assets/f8a15e2452febed245c0e0bc8a0e7cad8a3579f7205ec4a295fc36875b0d7141.png)\n\nThe solid enclosed by the hemisphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=16$ for $z\\ge 0,$ shown above, can be described in spherical polar coordinates as" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069421/assets/85ebe508035ae538d6becd73e9563be903a8cfcc4764550e131c35c84f7b80de.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:  \n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,4]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi /2]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi /2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361634 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fde60ded-ec05-5a2c-8844-26e8e02c9afb" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Part of a Sphere in Spherical Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f845b37e-a469-5a63-b8cd-66cdfc73e74d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 9,\\,\\pi /2\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi /2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361636} {:answer/id #uuid "92fbce80-ebd0-5021-a715-0dc15267520b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4,\\,\\pi /2\\le \\theta \\le \\pi /2,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361637} {:answer/id #uuid "70d86b5b-8e08-587b-8455-cc5d5b615338" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 6,\\,\\pi /2\\le \\theta \\le \\pi /2,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi /2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361638} {:answer/id #uuid "c4ff2aca-6caa-5e35-8db5-5cd7e33fd721" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 3,\\,\\pi /2\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le 2\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361639} {:answer/id #uuid "2e5fcc02-e966-52d7-86b0-24cc45be5775" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 6,\\,\\pi /2\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361640}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e5fcc02-e966-52d7-86b0-24cc45be5775" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 6,\\,\\pi /2\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361640} :answer-field/id #uuid "86ea7f3d-e895-5646-b10c-adb24a46e87c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361635}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d33696cd-4eae-5405-88b6-fb8354c01742" :question/math-academy-id "q-161822" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069421/assets/6b22ae2aaa00d077f8204fcfde073b22241debdce7ea7f7bb7bbfaf0ec213a5d.png)\n\nThe solid enclosed by the quarter-sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=36$ for $y\\ge 0$ and $x\\le 0,$ shown above, can be described in spherical polar coordinates as" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069421/assets/22c1271f2facac9a05548d4a94b5c41ecd68699f50af16f200687a2a3f94f1da.png)\n\n  \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary withing the following domains:\n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,6]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 6,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361641 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "49ec24aa-1f87-5ce1-8ae3-ae4cb5462ae6" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Part of a Shifted Sphere in Spherical Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96de6de2-0978-5064-a1f9-d9c36d4487e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361643} {:answer/id #uuid "4df0ce0a-2784-5ed1-98d7-dabe6b13640d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361644} {:answer/id #uuid "21d54970-20a9-5389-8e28-72b2265f3566" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361645} {:answer/id #uuid "480573a8-7d77-56e5-99eb-ce7dc6175b22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361646} {:answer/id #uuid "ce45b738-890b-5edd-a611-a68d82977b06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361647}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4df0ce0a-2784-5ed1-98d7-dabe6b13640d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361644} :answer-field/id #uuid "860a109d-2fba-5b4c-997e-048d856105e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361642}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "932b92db-8d77-5894-b632-3b8edb79745e" :question/math-academy-id "q-161883" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069421/assets/a32e8fd24c990a831cfee9525c6b1c035fb4ee6e09deedb3ac4c899d90ceeaac.png)\n\nThe solid enclosed by the half-sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4y=0$ for $z\\le 0,$ shown above, can be described in spherical polar coordinates as" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents one-half of the sphere of radius $2$ centered at $(0,-2,0).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4y=0$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4y & =0 \\\\ {\\rho }^{2}+4\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ & =0 \\\\ \\rho (\\rho +4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069421/assets/e2865e838521a8864ad76d72e520d4b128a8fceb8c18cb9c35ba10134419c4eb.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:\n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in [\\pi ,2\\pi ]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }.\n$$"}]}
